\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Link reflection as a trace square}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Use the [periodic gauge law](fdip-0083) with #{N=2m},
#{m\ge1}. Reflect time by #{\theta(j)=1-j} modulo #{2m};
the positive half is #{\{1,\ldots,m\}}. As in the
\vocabk{scalar definition}{fdip-007M}, this is a link reflection:
its cuts lie between times #{0,1} and #{m,m+1}.}

\p{Let #{\mathcal A_+} consist of finite linear combinations of
functions
##{F(U)=\prod_{j=1}^m f_j(U_j),}
where each #{f_j:X\to\mathbb C} is bounded, measurable and
gauge invariant. These form an algebra of spatial observables.
For such a product, on #{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}}} define
##{A_F=T_{\mathrm{phys}}^{1/2}M_{f_1}T_{\mathrm{phys}}
 M_{f_2}\cdots T_{\mathrm{phys}}M_{f_m}
 T_{\mathrm{phys}}^{1/2},}
where for #{m=1} this means
#{T_{\mathrm{phys}}^{1/2}M_{f_1}T_{\mathrm{phys}}^{1/2}}.
Extend this assignment linearly to finite sums. Then #{A_F} is
Hilbert–Schmidt and
##{\mathbb E_{2m}\left[\overline{F(\theta U)}F(U)\right]
 =\frac{\operatorname{Tr}(A_F^*A_F)}{Z_{2m}}\ge0,
 \qquad (\theta U)_j=U_{1-j}.}
The temporal links have already been integrated in the periodic
trace identity. No assertion about arbitrary observables involving
individual temporal links is included.}

\proof{
\p{The positive trace-class operator #{T_{\mathrm{phys}}} has a
Hilbert–Schmidt square root. Every multiplication operator here is
bounded, as are all intervening transfer factors, so #{A_F} is
Hilbert–Schmidt; the same holds for a finite sum.}

\p{For products #{F=\prod_j f_j(U_j)} and
#{G=\prod_j g_j(U_j)}, the adjoint reverses the order and
conjugates each multiplier. The [bounded trace formula](fdip-0083)
and cyclicity of a trace-class product give
##{\operatorname{Tr}(A_F^*A_G)
 =Z_{2m}\,\mathbb E_{2m}
   \left[\overline{F(\theta U)}G(U)\right].}
The cyclic order of the multipliers is
##{\overline f_m,\ldots,\overline f_1,g_1,\ldots,g_m,}
with one full transfer between adjacent entries. They occur at
times #{m+1,\ldots,2m,1,\ldots,m}, where #{2m=0}; hence
#{f_j} is conjugated at time #{1-j}. There are exactly #{2m}
transfer factors after the adjacent half-powers combine.}

\p{Expanding finite sums proves the same sesquilinear identity
for all #{F,G\in\mathcal A_+}. If two product-sum presentations
define the same function, their difference has zero expectation
against its reflected conjugate, and the identity makes the
corresponding difference of operators have Hilbert–Schmidt norm
zero. Thus #{A_F} is independent of that presentation. Taking
#{G=F} proves the trace-square identity and positivity.}
}

\p{This proof uses the strictly positive operator in the continuum
comparison of \ref{fdip-0080} and the exact finite-time law of
\ref{fdip-0083}.
It does not reverse the argument to reconstruct a transfer operator
from an arbitrary reflection-positive law; the
[scalar quotient obstruction](fdip-007Q) shows why that distinction
matters.}
